Rc8 { [%emt 0:00:47] [%think 3668] [%out 3920] [%in 2] [%cacheread 0]
[%cachewrite 1620] The main threat is Be3‑b6. The b6 square is covered by the
queen on f2 along the diagonal, so the bishop would hit my queen on c7 first
and then the rook on d8, and I'd lose the exchange. Qb7 looks natural but is
risky. After e5 dxe5 d6, the d-pawn advances and opens the long diagonal for
the white bishop on f3 onto b7, so my queen would be hit. Rc8 gets the rook off
the d-file and away from the Bb6 threat. On the c-file it also supports the
queen and the c4 pawn, preparing a later c3. If White plays f5, the knight can
go to e5. If White plays e5 dxe5 d6, my bishop on f8 can take d6. } 24. Bb6 Qb7
