b5 pawn hanging to the bishop. - **f6** is simplest. It fixes White's f5 pawn
and removes the fork for good. - If White breaks with e5, I can take on e5 and
win material. - The knight can later reroute via c8 to b6 to pressure d5. } 49.
Kg4 Nc8 { [%emt 0:00:55] [%think 4234] [%out 4598] [%in 2] [%cacheread 0]
[%cachewrite 2216] Black is up a piece for two pawns: bishop, knight and four
pawns against bishop and six pawns. The plan is to keep the extra piece and win
slowly. **Rejected captures:** - **Nxf5 or Bxf5+:** after exf5 each gives up a
piece for pawns, so both are unnecessary. - **Hitting d5:** attacking d5 twice
(for example Bc8–b7 plus the knight on e7) still costs a piece for two pawns,
because e4 recaptures. **Plan:** reroute the knight. The route e7–c8–b6–a4–c3
lets it attack e4. From c3 it can also jump to a2 and pick up the b4 pawn.
**White's replies are harmless:** - **Bxb5:** Bxb5 just loses White's bishop
for a pawn, since b5 is defended by the bishop on d7. - **The e5 break:** e5
fxe5 fxe5 dxe5 f6+ Kxf6 leaves Black comfortable. - **Kh5:** the White king has
no further way in. } 50. Kh5 Ne7 { [%emt 0:01:28] [%think 7128] [%out 7419]
[%in 2] [%cacheread 0] [%cachewrite 2242] The white king on h5 is almost boxed
in. g6 and h6 are covered by my king, g5 by the f6 pawn, and h4 is its own
pawn, so g4 is its only square. **Mating plan:** if my knight reaches h6 (or
e3) to cover g4, then Be8 is mate. White's bishop has almost no way to cover
the e8–h5 diagonal: it can't reach c4 (my b5 pawn covers it), e6 (my d7 bishop
covers it) or the g6 route (its own e4 pawn blocks it). The knight route is Ne7
→ g8 → h6. **Ne7 is also useful on its own:** - It hits the d5 and f5 pawns. -
It covers g6. - It stays far from White's e5 break. Bxb5 isn't a worry, because
my d7 bishop still protects b5. } 51. Bh3 Be8+ { [%emt 0:02:11] [%think 10712]
